%description:
Module test for TI-LFA (RFC 9855) backup activation against an ACTUAL live packet, rather than
the EV_INFO repair lines srmpls_tilfa_repair_pq.test/srmpls_tilfa_repair_bridge.test already
assert. Those two lock in the repair COMPUTATION and the activation/revert logging; this one
closes the remaining gap by pushing real traffic through the installed backup label stack, with
the narrowest observable that actually depends on it: the label stack Mpls::doStackOps() logs at
A's own LIB lookup for a live, in-flight packet.

Topology: hostA -- U -- A -- B -- D -- hostD is the primary path (metric 1 each, total 2), with
A -- E -- F -- D as the alternate, where the E-F link is EXPENSIVE (metric 5, so that path costs
7). U=9.9.9.9, A=1.1.1.1, B=2.2.2.2, D=4.4.4.4, E=5.5.5.5, F=6.6.6.6; srgbBase 16000. This is
srmpls_tilfa_repair_bridge.test's topology, metrics included, chosen deliberately -- the
asymmetric cost is what makes P-space and Q-space disjoint, and hence what makes the repair
carry a real label; see "what makes this discriminating" below.

U is the SR INGRESS: its SrPolicy classifies traffic to hostD's subnet onto the unconstrained
node-SID segment "node:4" (label 16004) and pushes it. A is deliberately kept OUT of that ingress
role and carries no policies of its own -- A is a pure TRANSIT hop for this flow (and the PLR
protecting the A-B link). This split matters: SrPolicy::resolveSegmentList() resolves its first
hop directly via SegmentRouting::resolveNextHop() and documents that "ingress-classified traffic
never consults the LIB at all" -- so if A itself were the ingress, the packets it originates would
never touch LibTable::resolveLabel(), and the backupActive branch this test exists to exercise
would silently never run. Only a packet arriving at A ALREADY label-encoded forces A to do a real
LIB lookup for label 16004, which is exactly where setBackup()/activateBackup() take effect.

What makes this discriminating: A's repair for D here is a genuine EXTRA push. No PQ node exists
(P-space and Q-space do not overlap), so findTiLfaRepair() bridges with the two segments [E, F];
E is A's own next hop over the repair path, so its segment is dropped (A is E's penultimate hop
and PHP means E never expects its own node-SID -- see SegmentRouting::encodeRepair()), leaving a
single PUSH of F's node-SID 16006 riding on top of the packet's still-present inner 16004. That
push is something ordinary node-SID forwarding at A never produces: recomputeAndInstall(), which
runs on the same event that detects the failure, reprograms A's node-SID(D) entry to SWAP 16004
toward E and would never push 16006 there. So "doStackOps: {PUSH 16006}" in A's log can only come
from LibTable::resolveLabel() taking its backupActive branch for a real packet.

This is also why the topology is NOT a symmetric A-{B,C}-D diamond: there the repair's only
segment would be A's own directly-connected neighbour, which PHP requires be dropped, collapsing
the repair to exactly the SWAP ordinary reconvergence installs -- correct, but indistinguishable
from no TI-LFA at all, so it could not carry this assertion.

At t=1s a ~ScenarioManager severs the A-B link; at t=2s it is restored. Traffic runs continuously
across both transitions, and every repaired packet is expected to arrive: the repair path is a
working path, not a black hole.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.mpls.SrMplsRouter;
import inet.common.scenario.ScenarioManager;
import ned.DatarateChannel;

network TiLfaTrafficTest
{
    parameters:
        **.ipv4.configurator.networkConfiguratorModule = "";
    types:
        channel Link extends DatarateChannel
        {
            datarate = 1Mbps;
            delay = 10us;
        }
    submodules:
        scenarioManager: ScenarioManager;
        hostA: StandardHost {
            @display("p=50,150");
        }
        U: SrMplsRouter {
            parameters:
                routerId = "9.9.9.9";
                peers = "ppp1"; // ppp0 goes to hostA, not an IGP peer
                sidTable = xmldoc("sidtable.xml");
                policies = xmldoc("policiesU.xml");
                @display("p=180,150");
            gates:
                pppg[2];
        }
        A: SrMplsRouter {
            parameters:
                routerId = "1.1.1.1";
                peers = "ppp0 ppp1 ppp2";
                sidTable = xmldoc("sidtable.xml");
                segmentRouting.tiLfa = true;
                @display("p=310,150");
            gates:
                pppg[3];
        }
        B: SrMplsRouter {
            parameters:
                routerId = "2.2.2.2";
                peers = "ppp0 ppp1";
                sidTable = xmldoc("sidtable.xml");
                @display("p=440,80");
            gates:
                pppg[2];
        }
        E: SrMplsRouter {
            parameters:
                routerId = "5.5.5.5";
                peers = "ppp0 ppp1";
                sidTable = xmldoc("sidtable.xml");
                @display("p=310,250");
            gates:
                pppg[2];
        }
        F: SrMplsRouter {
            parameters:
                routerId = "6.6.6.6";
                peers = "ppp0 ppp1";
                sidTable = xmldoc("sidtable.xml");
                @display("p=450,250");
            gates:
                pppg[2];
        }
        D: SrMplsRouter {
            parameters:
                routerId = "4.4.4.4";
                peers = "ppp0 ppp1"; // ppp2 goes to hostD, not an IGP peer
                sidTable = xmldoc("sidtable.xml");
                @display("p=570,150");
            gates:
                pppg[3];
        }
        hostD: StandardHost {
            @display("p=700,150");
        }
    connections:
        hostA.pppg++ <--> Link <--> U.pppg[0];
        U.pppg[1] <--> Link <--> A.pppg[0];
        A.pppg[1] <--> Link <--> B.pppg[0]; // primary: A -> B -> D, 2 hops
        B.pppg[1] <--> Link <--> D.pppg[0];
        A.pppg[2] <--> Link <--> E.pppg[0]; // alternate: A -> E -> F -> D, 3 hops
        E.pppg[1] <--> Link <--> F.pppg[0];
        F.pppg[1] <--> Link <--> D.pppg[1];
        D.pppg[2] <--> Link <--> hostD.pppg++;
}

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: sidtable.xml
<sr>
    <node router="1.1.1.1" sid="1"/>
    <node router="2.2.2.2" sid="2"/>
    <node router="4.4.4.4" sid="4"/>
    <node router="5.5.5.5" sid="5"/>
    <node router="6.6.6.6" sid="6"/>
</sr>

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: policiesU.xml
<policies>
    <policy dest="10.0.9.30" netmask="255.255.255.255" segments="node:4"/>
</policies>

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: scenario.xml
<scenario>
    <at t="1s">
        <disconnect src-module="A" src-gate="pppg$o[1]"/>
        <disconnect src-module="B" src-gate="pppg$o[0]"/>
    </at>
    <at t="2s">
        <connect src-module="A" src-gate="pppg[1]"
                 dest-module="B" dest-gate="pppg[0]"
                 channel-type="ned.DatarateChannel">
            <param name="delay" value="10us"/>
            <param name="datarate" value="1Mbps"/>
        </connect>
    </at>
</scenario>

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: hostA.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.1	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

route:
default:	10.0.9.2	0.0.0.0		G	0	ppp0
routeend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: U.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.2	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp1	inet_addr: 10.0.9.3	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: A.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.4	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp1	inet_addr: 10.0.9.5	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp2	inet_addr: 10.0.9.7	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: B.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.6	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp1	inet_addr: 10.0.9.9	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: E.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.8	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp1	inet_addr: 10.0.9.14	MTU: 1500	Metric: 5
ifconfigend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: F.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.15	MTU: 1500	Metric: 5
name: ppp1	inet_addr: 10.0.9.12	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: D.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.10	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp1	inet_addr: 10.0.9.11	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
name: ppp2	inet_addr: 10.0.9.13	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: hostD.rt
ifconfig:
name: ppp0	inet_addr: 10.0.9.30	MTU: 1500	Metric: 1
ifconfigend.

route:
default:	10.0.9.13	0.0.0.0		G	0	ppp0
routeend.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
ned-path = .;../../../../src
network = TiLfaTrafficTest
sim-time-limit = 3s
total-stack = 64MiB
record-vector-results = false
cmdenv-express-mode = false
**.cmdenv-log-level = "info"
output-scalar-file = "${resultdir}/${configname}-#${runnumber}.sca"

**.hostA.numApps = 1
**.hostA.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
**.hostA.app[0].localPort = 100
**.hostA.app[0].destPort = 100
**.hostA.app[0].messageLength = 128 bytes
**.hostA.app[0].startTime = 0.2s
**.hostA.app[0].sendInterval = 0.01s
**.hostA.app[0].destAddresses = "hostD"

**.hostD.numApps = 1
**.hostD.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
**.hostD.app[0].localPort = 100

**.hostA.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "hostA.rt"
**.hostD.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "hostD.rt"
**.U.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "U.rt"
**.A.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "A.rt"
**.B.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "B.rt"
**.E.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "E.rt"
**.F.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "F.rt"
**.D.ipv4.routingTable.routingFile = "D.rt"

**.ppp[*].queue.typename = "DropTailQueue"
**.ppp[*].queue.packetCapacity = 10

**.scenarioManager.script = xmldoc("scenario.xml")

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# Before the failure: U's SrPolicy ingress push puts the unconstrained node-SID (D=16004) on
%# every packet, exactly as sr_reroute.test's identical setup shows
%contains: stdout
doStackOps: {PUSH 16004}
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# The failure is actually injected
%contains: stdout
Disconnecting TiLfaTrafficTest.A.pppg$o[1]
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# TI-LFA activates the precomputed backup for D as soon as A's link to B (2.2.2.2) dies
%contains: stdout
TI-LFA: activated backup for 4.4.4.4 (protected link to 2.2.2.2 went down)
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# The discriminating assertion: a LIVE, already label-encoded packet arriving at A (a genuine
%# TRANSIT lookup, since U -- not A -- is the SR ingress) gets an outer PUSH of F's node-SID
%# (16006) stacked on top of the still-present inner 16004. Ordinary (non-backup) node-SID
%# forwarding at A never does this: after reconvergence that entry SWAPs 16004 toward E and
%# never pushes 16006. This can only happen via LibTable::resolveLabel()'s backupActive branch
%# actually being taken for a real packet.
%contains: stdout
doStackOps: {PUSH 16006}
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# ... and reverts once the A-B link comes back up
%contains: stdout
TI-LFA: reverted backup for 4.4.4.4 (protected link to 2.2.2.2 came back up)
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# The repair path actually carries the traffic: no packet is dropped for want of a LIB entry.
%# This is what distinguishes a working repair from one that merely computes and installs a
%# label stack the next hop cannot resolve (which would silently black-hole the whole failure
%# window while every log assertion above still passed).
%postrun-command: grep -c "incoming label not resolved" test.out > unresolved.out || true
%contains: unresolved.out
0
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# Traffic keeps flowing throughout: hostD receives a large, still-climbing count by the end
%postrun-command: grep "TiLfaTrafficTest.hostD.app\[0\] packetReceived:count" results/General-#0.sca | awk '{ if ($NF+0 > 200) print "HOSTD_KEEPS_FLOWING", $0 }' > hostd.out
%contains: hostd.out
HOSTD_KEEPS_FLOWING
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%postrun-command: grep "undisposed object:" test.out > test_undisposed.out || true
%not-contains: test_undisposed.out
undisposed object: (
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
